Z||000029||goldiron||08-18-2001||12:56 AM||||I don't see how Pontiac can exit the muscle car arena without following Olds to the exit. Let's face it, without the Firebird, why buy a Pontiac?  Options are:<BR>  1. The firebird doesn't disappear<BR>  2. Firebird exists and enter the new Fiero.<BR>  3. Pontiac develops vision and keeps one and brings back the other.<P>Two positives about a new fiero.<BR>   1. Following the move to retro look, we can drop the excessive body curves re the Opel.  Don't want the new fiero to look 'common'.  This will allow for the new fiero to retain some semblance of original styling.<BR>   2. Most potential buyers are too young to remember the 'high temperature?' problems of early versions.  GM just needs to buy enough ads space to keep media silent on the history.<BR>   3. With 17 years and millions of fiero road miles in the books, they should be able to make a near bullet proof fiero with a pace setting warranty.<P>Me - I get Ponitac excitement thinking about the possibilities here.  Just hope Pontiac won't mess up second time around.<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000030||GreeBo||08-18-2001||08:27 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Yellow88GT:<BR><B>Unfortunately, I think a $20,000 price tag<BR>for a Lotus based car is wishful thinking.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Not necessarily.  The MR2 was a lotus based car from the ground up and it was about 15k in 1987.  Today that might be more like 25k but thats not bad considering all the perks that come with Lotus engineering.<P><P>------------------<BR>GreeBo<BR>1987 MR2-T N/A (K&N/Plugs/Exhst Modded)||||reg||1
